# Reviewable pull requests

A PR is reviewable when one person can check it properly in one sitting.
Defect detection falls off as review size grows (SmartBear/Cisco study:
sharply above a few hundred lines), so size is a correctness concern, not style.

Budget, counted as `git diff --stat` against the base, excluding lockfiles,
generated code, and vendored files: **≤ 500 changed lines**. Smaller is better;
500 is a ceiling, not a target.

- One logical change per PR. If the title needs "and", split it.
- Never mix a refactor, rename, or move with a behaviour change. Land the
  mechanical change first, then the behaviour change on top.
- No drive-by edits (formatting, unrelated fixes, dependency bumps). Note them
  in the PR body as follow-ups instead.

When a task exceeds the budget, stack it: plan the sequence of PRs first,
make each one build and pass tests on its own, and base each branch on the
previous one. Prefer landing an unused-but-tested piece before the change that
wires it in.

## Oversize overrides

Exceeding 500 lines is allowed only when splitting would leave an intermediate
state that doesn't build, is incorrect, or is harder to review than the whole.
Allowed categories:

- **Mechanical**: rename, move, format, or codemod. State the exact command
  that produced it, so review means rerunning it and checking the diff matches.
  It contains no hand edits; put any fix-ups in a separate commit.
- **Deletion-only**: removal of dead code with no behaviour change.
- **Atomic**: a change that must land together, such as an interface change
  and all its callers, or a schema migration and the code that reads it.

An oversize PR starts its body with
`Oversize: <category> — <why it can't be split>`. It must also list what to
read closely and what can be skimmed. "It was faster" is not a reason.

## PR body

Start the body from `.github/pull_request_template.md`. Its headings are the
text before the colon in each item below, in this order. Keep each section no
longer than it needs to be, and write `None` in a section that has nothing
rather than removing it.

1. What changes and why: 2–4 sentences. Link the issue or design doc. When the
   work has a Linear issue, include `Fixes <issue ID>` (for example
   `Fixes ENG-123`), which links the PR to the issue.
2. How to review: where to start reading, what is mechanical and can be skimmed.
3. How it was verified: commands run and their results, and which test covers
   each acceptance criterion.
4. What was not verified, and known limitations: include deliberate omissions.
5. Changes from the plan, and problems found outside the scope: how the work
   differs from the agreed plan. Report problems outside the task's scope as
   new issues and link them, rather than fixing them in this PR.
6. Diagrams: `updated` (name which ones) or `no structural change`. If the PR
   changes components, data flow, state, or external dependencies, update
   `docs/architecture.md` in the same PR, following the `architecture-docs` skill.
7. Risks and follow-ups: a breaking change lists a follow-up `prune-review` of
   the affected area.

Commit history should read as the review narrative: each commit is one
coherent step with a message stating why, not just what.
